GAD.reg("lesson","fm.intro.role",{"id":"fm.intro.role","title":"The finance function, the financial manager and the controller","md":"## In plain words\n\n### One tapri, three questions\n\nTwenty years ago Tapri Tea Co. was one roadside stall in Indore. Before the first cup was sold, Kesar had to answer four questions: which things to buy (a stove, a cart, kettles), how much they would cost in all, how much cash he would need every day for milk, tea leaves and his helper's wages, and where all that money would come from (his savings, and a loan from his uncle at 2% a month).\n\n> **Kesar:** Back then I did all of it myself, on the back of a matchbox.\n> **Dev:** And today the same questions need a CFO, a treasurer, a controller and ten managers.\n> **Kesar:** Same questions? Then why are you so expensive?\n> **Dev:** Because the matchbox had one stall. We have 40 cities.\n\nThose questions, and one more that arrived with the first profit (how much of it to take home), are the whole of **financial management**: getting money (the **financing decision**), putting it to work in assets (the **investment decision**), and deciding how much of the profit to hand back to the owners (the **dividend decision**). Running the daily cash, stock and credit is **working capital management**. Together these are the **finance functions**, and the value of the firm depends on how well all of them are taken.\n\nMoney comes from different sources, each with its own **risk, cost and control**: owners' equity is safe for the firm but expensive; debentures are cheaper but must be repaid with interest come what may. And money raised is only useful if it earns more than it costs, so the finance manager also watches how it is used: no idle cash, no excess stock, no machine that cannot pay its way.\n\nThe person in charge has grown too. Once the finance department was called in only for big events (a merger, an expansion). Today the **chief financial officer (CFO)** is a strategic partner of the chief executive, and under the CFO sit a **treasurer** (who raises, invests and guards the money) and a **controller** (who accounts for it, costs it, taxes it and audits it).\n\n::: analogy The town's water engineer\nA town's water engineer brings water in from the river, the borewell and the tanker, each with its own price and reliability (financing); pipes it to the fields and factories that will make the best use of it (investment); and decides how much to release to households now and how much to keep in the dam for next summer (dividend versus retention). A leaking pipe is idle money: water paid for that waters nothing.\n:::\n\n::: key\nFinancial management is the efficient acquisition (financing) and allocation (investment) of funds, with the dividend decision on top: three inter-related long-term decisions plus working capital management. The finance executive who takes them has grown from a fund-raiser for big events into the CEO's strategic partner.\n:::\n\n## How it works\n\n### 1. Four stages before the first sale\n\nThe Study Material opens with the four stages of decision making every entrepreneur goes through (SM 1.2):\n\n::: diagram An entrepreneur's four decisions\n{\"type\": \"flow\", \"w\": 400, \"nodes\": [\n {\"id\": \"s1\", \"kind\": \"start\", \"label\": \"1. Which assets\\nto buy\"},\n {\"id\": \"s2\", \"kind\": \"process\", \"label\": \"2. Total investment\\nneeded for them\"},\n {\"id\": \"s3\", \"kind\": \"process\", \"label\": \"3. Cash to run daily\\noperations\"},\n {\"id\": \"s4\", \"kind\": \"end\", \"label\": \"4. Sources to finance\\nthe total\"}],\n \"edges\": [[\"s1\", \"s2\"], [\"s2\", \"s3\", \"working capital\"], [\"s3\", \"s4\", \"assets + working capital\"]],\n \"caption\": \"Stage 1: premises, machinery, equipment. Stage 3 is the working capital requirement (raw material, salaries, wages). Stage 4: share capital, including the entrepreneur's own funds, borrowing from banks, or investment from financial institutions.\"}\n:::\n\nWhile deciding how much to take from each source, the entrepreneur keeps in mind the **cost of capital** of each source (interest, dividend) and tries to keep it low. So financial management is concerned with ==efficient acquisition (financing) and allocation (investment in assets, working capital etc.) of funds== with an objective to make profit (dividend) for the owners. Any business requiring money asks three key questions (SM 1.3):\n\n1. Where to get the money from? (**Financing decision**)\n2. Where to invest the money? (**Investment decision**)\n3. How much to distribute amongst shareholders to keep them satisfied? (**Dividend decision**)\n\n### 2. What financial management means\n\n::: definition Financial management\nThat managerial activity which is concerned with planning and controlling of the firm's financial resources; in other words, acquiring, financing and managing assets to accomplish the overall goal of a business enterprise (mainly to maximise the shareholder's wealth). (SM 1.3)\n:::\n\nWhere positive cash flow matters more than book profit, it can also be defined as planning for the future of a business enterprise to ensure a positive cash flow; some experts call it the science of money management. Two formal definitions (SM 1.3–1.4):\n\n- \"Financial Management comprises of forecasting, planning, organizing, directing, co-ordinating and controlling of all activities relating to acquisition and application of the financial resources of an undertaking in keeping with its financial objective.\"\n- **Phillippatus**: \"Financial Management is concerned with the managerial decisions that result in the acquisition and financing of short term and long term credits for the firm.\" It deals with the selection of specific assets (or combinations of assets) and the size and growth of an enterprise, and the analysis is based on the expected inflows and outflows of funds and their effect on managerial objectives.\n\n### 3. The two aspects: getting funds and using them\n\nThere are two basic aspects of financial management: **procurement of funds** and the **effective utilisation** of these funds to achieve business objectives (SM 1.4).\n\n::: diagram Two aspects of financial management\n{\"type\": \"nest\", \"w\": 400, \"root\": {\"title\": \"Financial management\", \"dir\": \"h\", \"color\": \"mute\", \"children\": [\n {\"title\": \"Procurement of funds\", \"mono\": false, \"color\": \"a\", \"lines\": [\"Owner's funds (equity)\", \"Debentures and bonds\", \"Commercial banks\", \"International funding\", \"Angel financing\", \"Venture capital\"]},\n {\"title\": \"Utilisation of funds\", \"mono\": false, \"color\": \"g\", \"lines\": [\"Fixed assets\", \"(capital budgeting)\", \"Working capital\", \"(inventories, book\", \"debts, cash)\"]}]}}\n:::\n\n**Procurement.** Because funds can be obtained from many sources, procurement is always a complex problem (SM 1.4). New and creative sources keep appearing (trading in **carbon credits** is one, SM 1.5). Funds from different sources have different characteristics in terms of **risk, cost and control**: the cost of funds should be at the minimum, and for that a proper balancing of risk and control must be carried out; the finance manager must also strike a balance between equity and debt so that the funding structure suits the business (SM 1.5).\n\n| Source | From the firm's point of view (SM 1.5–1.6) |\n|---|---|\n| **Equity** | Best from the risk point of view: no repayment except in liquidation. Usually the most expensive: shareholders expect more than the prevalent interest rate, and dividends are an appropriation of profit, not an expense under the Income Tax Act. A public issue may dilute existing control. |\n| **Debentures** | Comparatively cheaper than shares because of their tax advantage: interest is allowed for tax, a dividend comes out of taxed profit. But interest must be paid even in hard times, whether or not there are profits, and the debentures must be repaid as agreed: a high degree of risk. |\n| **Banks** | Meet long-term and short-term needs. Fund-based: cash credit, overdraft, term loans, working capital term loan, bill purchase/discounting. Non-fund-based: guarantees, letters of credit. |\n| **International funding** | FDI and FII are the two major routes, besides ADRs and GDRs; procurement must suit the requirements of foreign investors. |\n| **Angel financing** | Equity from a wealthy individual for a start-up or expansion; sometimes the last option for a start-up that does not qualify for bank funding and is too small for venture capital. |\n\nThe sources are taught in [[fm.sources.long|long-term sources]], [[fm.sources.short|short-term sources]] and [[fm.sources.modern|contemporary and international sources]].\n\n**Utilisation.** The finance manager must point out where funds are idle or not properly used. All funds are procured at a cost and with some risk; if they do not generate an income higher than the cost of procuring them, there is no point in running the business (SM 1.7). Funds go to:\n\n- **Fixed assets**: invested so that the company can produce at its optimum level without endangering its financial solvency. This needs sound knowledge of **capital budgeting** (investment appraisal), the planning process used to decide whether long-term investments such as new or replacement machinery, new plants, new products and research projects would provide the desired return (SM 1.7; [[fm.capbud.cashflows|capital budgeting]]).\n- **Working capital**: enough of it, but not too much blocked in inventories, book debts and cash (SM 1.7; [[fm.wc.basics|working capital]]).\n\n### 4. The finance functions: V = f(I, F, D)\n\nThe value of a firm depends on its finance functions or decisions (SM 1.8):\n\n$$V = f(I, F, D)$$\n\n::: diagram The finance functions\n{\"type\": \"tree\", \"gapX\": 14, \"root\": {\"label\": \"Finance functions\", \"children\": [\n {\"label\": \"Long-term\", \"children\": [{\"label\": \"Investment\\n(I)\", \"color\": \"a\"}, {\"label\": \"Financing\\n(F)\", \"color\": \"b\"}, {\"label\": \"Dividend\\n(D)\", \"color\": \"c\"}]},\n {\"label\": \"Short-term\", \"children\": [{\"label\": \"Working\\ncapital\", \"color\": \"g\"}]}]},\n \"caption\": \"The three long-term decisions decide V = f(I, F, D); short-term decisions reduce to working capital management (current assets and current liabilities).\"}\n:::\n\n**(a) Investment decisions (I)** relate to the **selection of assets** in which funds will be invested. Long-term funds go into fixed assets and also current assets, after careful assessment of the projects through capital budgeting; a part of long-term funds is kept for working capital. Asset management policies are laid down for current assets: the **inventory policy** is decided by the production manager and the finance manager in view of production needs, future raw material prices and the availability of funds (SM 1.8).\n\n**(b) Financing decisions (F)** relate to **acquiring the optimum finance** to meet financial objectives and seeing that fixed and working capital are effectively managed. The finance manager needs (SM 1.8–1.9):\n\n- a good knowledge of the sources of funds and their costs, and a **sound capital structure**: a proper balance between equity capital and debt;\n- a clear understanding of the difference between **profit and cash flow**: profit is of little avail unless the organisation has cash to pay for assets and sustain the working capital cycle;\n- the evaluation of **risk**: excessive debt carries high risk for equity because of the priority rights of lenders;\n- in overseas trading, protection against currency fluctuations by **hedging**, a strategy designed to minimise, reduce or cancel out the risk in another investment (as a shopkeeper hedges the risk of fire with a fire insurance contract).\n\n**(c) Dividend decisions (D)** relate to **how much and how frequently cash can be paid out** of the profits as income for the owners. An owner is rewarded in two ways, the growth of the capital invested and the cash paid out (drawings for a sole trader, dividends for a company). The dividend decision has two elements: the amount paid out and the amount retained to support growth, ==the latter being also a financing decision==. The level and regular growth of dividends is a significant factor in a company's market value (SM 1.9; [[fm.div.basics|dividend policy]]).\n\nAll three are inter-related. The first two arise in any organisation; the dividend decision only in profit-making ones (SM 1.9).\n\n**Short-term finance decisions** are generally reduced to the management of current assets and current liabilities: **working capital management (WCM)** (SM 1.10).\n\n### 5. Why the three decisions must be taken together\n\nICAI's Suggested Answers explain the inter-relationship this way (MTP Series II for May 2024 and for September 2026, Q4(a)): the underlying objective of all three is the same, the **maximisation of shareholders' wealth**, so their joint impact on the market price of the share must be considered and they should be solved jointly.\n\n::: diagram How the three decisions pull on one another\n{\"type\": \"graph\", \"w\": 360, \"h\": 270, \"nodes\": [\n {\"id\": \"I\", \"label\": \"Investment\", \"x\": 180, \"y\": 45, \"fill\": \"a\"},\n {\"id\": \"F\", \"label\": \"Financing\", \"x\": 70, \"y\": 220, \"fill\": \"b\"},\n {\"id\": \"D\", \"label\": \"Dividend\", \"x\": 290, \"y\": 220, \"fill\": \"c\"}],\n \"edges\": [\n  {\"from\": \"I\", \"to\": \"F\", \"label\": \"needs finance\", \"curve\": 0.35},\n  {\"from\": \"F\", \"to\": \"I\", \"label\": \"return ≥ cost\", \"curve\": 0.35},\n  {\"from\": \"D\", \"to\": \"F\", \"label\": \"retention funds it\"}],\n \"caption\": \"A new project needs finance; it is accepted only if its return at least covers the cost of that finance; and retained earnings used for internal financing deprive shareholders of dividends.\"}\n:::\n\n- **Investment**: long-term funds are invested after careful assessment through capital budgeting and uncertainty analysis, and only a proposal expected to yield at least enough to meet **its cost of financing** is accepted.\n- **Financing**: a proper balance between long-term and short-term funds, and within long-term funds a proper mix of loan funds and owners' funds; the optimum mix increases the return to equity shareholders.\n- **Dividend**: what part of profit to pay out and what to retain; an optimal dividend pay-out ratio maximises shareholders' wealth.\n\n### 6. Importance and scope\n\nThe importance of financial management cannot be over-emphasised: it is the key to successful business operations, and without proper administration of finance no business enterprise can reach its full potential for growth and success. \"Money is to an enterprise, what oil is to an engine.\" Financial management is all about planning investment, funding the investment, monitoring expenses against budget and managing gains from the investments; it means the management of all matters related to an organisation's finances (SM 1.10). The best way to show its importance is to list the tasks good financial management involves (SM 1.10):\n\n- Taking care not to over-invest in fixed assets\n- Balancing cash outflows with cash inflows\n- Ensuring a sufficient level of short-term working capital\n- Setting sales revenue targets that will deliver growth\n- Increasing gross profit by setting the correct pricing for products or services\n- Controlling general and administrative expenses by finding more cost-efficient ways of running day-to-day operations\n- Tax planning that will minimise the taxes the business has to pay\n\n**Scope.** Based on **Ezra Solomon's** concept, financial management studies (SM 1.11):\n\n1. Determination of the size of the enterprise and its rate of growth.\n2. Determining the composition of its assets.\n3. Determining the mix of its financing, i.e. the level of debt to equity.\n4. Analysis, planning and control of its financial affairs.\n\n### 7. Three phases of evolution\n\nFinancial management evolved as a separate field of study at the beginning of the century and gradually over the past 50 years, in three phases (SM 1.7–1.8):\n\n::: diagram Evolution of financial management\n{\"type\": \"layers\", \"layers\": [\n {\"label\": \"Traditional phase\", \"note\": \"only occasional events, e.g. mergers, liquidation\"},\n {\"label\": \"Transitional phase\", \"note\": \"day-to-day funds analysis, planning, control\"},\n {\"label\": \"Modern phase\", \"note\": \"still going on: analysis and new theories\"}],\n \"caption\": \"Top to bottom: the three phases in order (SM 1.7–1.8).\"}\n:::\n\n- **Traditional phase**: financial management was considered necessary only during occasional events such as takeovers, mergers, expansion and liquidation, and financial decisions kept in mind the needs of outsiders (investment bankers, lenders and the like).\n- **Transitional phase**: the day-to-day problems financial managers faced were given importance: funds analysis, planning and control.\n- **Modern phase**: still going on. The scope has greatly increased; financial analysis helps decision making, and many theories have been developed on efficient markets, capital budgeting, option pricing, valuation models and other fields.\n\n### 8. The role of the financial controller\n\nThe role of the **financial controller** has changed over the years. Until the middle of the century (in the Study Material's words), its scope was limited to **procurement of funds** under major events in the life of the enterprise such as promotion, expansion or merger. In modern times it includes, besides procurement of funds, the three decisions: investment, financing and dividend (SM 1.11).\n\n::: diagram An overview of the financial controller\n{\"type\": \"layers\", \"layers\": [\n {\"label\": \"Financial controller\"},\n {\"label\": \"Maximisation of share value\", \"note\": \"the goal\"},\n {\"label\": \"Financial decisions\", \"note\": \"financing, investment, dividend\"},\n {\"label\": \"Trade-off\", \"note\": \"return against risk\"}],\n \"caption\": \"The Study Material's figure links the market value, the three financial decisions and the risk-return trade-off (SM 1.12).\"}\n:::\n\nIn a bid to maximise shareholders' wealth, the financial controller should ==strive to maximise returns in relation to the given risk==, and seek courses of action that avoid unnecessary risks. To ensure maximum return, funds flowing in and out of the firm should be constantly monitored to ensure that they are safeguarded and properly utilised (SM 1.11).\n\n### 9. The finance executive and the CFO\n\nWith liberalisation, deregulation and globalisation, the opportunities before finance managers have virtually no limits; the finance executive goes by different names in different organisations, but the role and functions are similar (SM 1.18). ICAI quotes Jeff Thomson, IMA President and CEO: today's CFO team is expected to add value well beyond the traditional roles of cost management, controls and acting as the conscience of the organisation, by serving as the **integration hub** for key business processes, a **catalyst for change** including business transformation, and a **consultant or trusted business advisor** in creating sustainable growth (SM 1.19).\n\nThe finance executive's responsibilities include (SM 1.19):\n\n1. **Financial analysis and planning**: determining the proper amount of funds to employ in the firm, i.e. designating the size of the firm and its rate of growth.\n2. **Investment decisions**: the efficient allocation of funds to specific assets.\n3. **Financing and capital structure decisions**: raising funds on terms as favourable as possible, i.e. determining the composition of liabilities.\n4. **Management of financial resources** (such as working capital).\n5. **Risk management**: protecting assets.\n\nIn a large organisation the finance function may be organised under the CFO in two arms (SM 1.19):\n\n::: diagram Organisation of the finance function\n{\"type\": \"nest\", \"w\": 400, \"root\": {\"title\": \"Chief Financial Officer / Finance Executive\", \"dir\": \"h\", \"color\": \"mute\", \"children\": [\n {\"title\": \"Treasurer\", \"mono\": false, \"color\": \"a\", \"lines\": [\"Cash manager\", \"Credit manager\", \"Capital budgeting manager\", \"Fund raising manager\", \"Portfolio manager\"]},\n {\"title\": \"Controller\", \"mono\": false, \"color\": \"d\", \"lines\": [\"Financial accounting manager\", \"Cost accounting manager\", \"Tax manager\", \"Data processing manager\", \"Internal auditor\"]}]},\n \"caption\": \"Blue: the treasurer's side, which raises, invests and safeguards the money. Orange: the controller's side, which records, costs, taxes and checks it.\"}\n:::\n\n**Today's CFO.** The role is no longer confined to accounting, financial reporting and risk management: it is about being a **strategic business partner of the CEO** (SM 1.20).\n\n| What a CFO used to do | What a CFO now does |\n|---|---|\n| Budgeting | Budgeting |\n| Forecasting | Forecasting |\n| Accounting | Managing M&As |\n| Treasury (cash management) | Profitability analysis (by customer or product) |\n| Preparing internal financial reports for management | Pricing analysis |\n| Preparing quarterly and annual filings for investors | Decisions about outsourcing |\n| Tax filing | Overseeing the IT function |\n| Tracking accounts payable and accounts receivable | Overseeing the HR function |\n| Travel and entertainment expense management | Strategic planning (sometimes overseeing it) |\n| | Regulatory compliance |\n| | Risk management |\n\n## Worked examples\n\n:::: example One export plant, three decisions\nTapri's board is considering a ₹75 crore plant at Indore to make frozen samosas for export. Dev expects it to earn 16% a year; Tapri's cost of capital is 12%. He proposes to fund it with ₹25 crore of this year's profit, cutting this year's dividend from ₹32 crore to ₹7 crore, and ₹50 crore by either 9% non-convertible debentures or a rights issue of equity shares. A director asks why the board cannot simply approve the plant today and \"sort out the money and the dividend later\".\n\nExplain to the director, with reference to this proposal, how the investment, financing and dividend decisions are inter-related. (4 marks)\n\n::: answer Model answer\n**Inter-relationship.** The finance functions are divided into three major decisions: investment, financing and dividend. They are inter-related because the underlying objective of all three is the same, the maximisation of shareholders' wealth. Since they are inter-related, their joint impact on the market price of the company's shares must be considered, and they should be solved jointly.\n\n**Investment decision.** The plant is a long-term investment to be assessed through capital budgeting. It should be accepted only if it is expected to yield at least a return adequate to meet its cost of financing. The expected 16% exceeds the 12% cost of capital, but the cost depends on how the ₹75 crore is raised, so the investment cannot be approved without the financing decision.\n\n**Financing decision.** The finance manager has to ensure a proper mix of loan funds and owners' funds. Raising ₹50 crore through 9% debentures is cheaper because interest is tax-deductible, but it adds fixed interest and repayment risk; a rights issue avoids that risk but is costlier. The optimum mix increases the return to equity shareholders and maximises their wealth.\n\n**Dividend decision.** Retaining ₹25 crore of profit is internal financing, so it is also a financing decision: it deprives shareholders of ₹25 crore of dividend now in exchange for growth. The level and regular growth of dividends affect the market price of the share, so the pay-out must be set together with the plant and its financing.\n\n**Conclusion.** The three decisions are inter-related and must be taken jointly, judging each by its effect on shareholders' wealth. The board should approve the plant, its financing mix and the dividend together.\n:::\n::::\n\n:::: example Which decision is it?\nIdentify the finance decision (investment, financing, dividend or working capital management) involved in each of these Tapri proposals, giving reasons:\n\n(a) Choosing between one ₹12 crore fully automatic kulhad kiln and two semi-automatic kilns.\n\n(b) Buying US dollars forward to pay, in six months, for espresso machines bought from Italy.\n\n(c) Paying only 30% of this year's profit as dividend so that the rest can fund the delivery app.\n\n(d) Cutting the credit period for corporate catering clients from 60 days to 30 days.\n\n(e) Deciding how many months of Assam tea leaves to stock ahead of an expected price rise, together with the production head.\n\n(f) Choosing between ₹100 crore of debentures and ₹50 crore each of debentures and equity for a new plant.\n\n::: answer Model answer\n| Proposal | Decision | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| (a) | Investment | Selection of the asset in which funds will be invested, appraised by capital budgeting. |\n| (b) | Financing | Overseas trading exposes the firm to currency fluctuations; hedging the risk is part of the financing decision (SM 1.9). |\n| (c) | Dividend, and also financing | How much to pay out and how much to retain; the retained part finances growth, so it is also a financing decision (SM 1.9). |\n| (d) | Working capital management | A short-term decision on a current asset (receivables). |\n| (e) | Investment | An asset management policy for a current asset: the inventory policy, set by the production manager and the finance manager in view of production needs, future raw material prices and availability of funds (SM 1.8). |\n| (f) | Financing | The mix of debt and equity: a sound capital structure. |\n\nOn (e): the Study Material places the inventory policy under the investment decision (asset management policies for current assets, SM 1.8), so write \"investment\"; a line adding that managing the stock day to day is part of working capital management shows you know where both sit.\n:::\n::::\n\n::::: example Treasurer or controller?\nTapri's new CFO is organising the finance function as the Study Material suggests. Ten jobs are on her list:\n\n1. Tracking the cash position of 40 cities every morning\n2. Negotiating a ₹200 crore term loan\n3. Appraising the export plant's NPV\n4. Checking a new corporate client's creditworthiness\n5. Investing surplus cash in liquid funds and bonds\n6. Preparing the quarterly financial statements\n7. Working out the cost of one kulhad of chai\n8. Filing GST and income-tax returns\n9. Running the system that records every café's sales\n10. Testing whether café cash controls work\n\nShow how the finance function may be organised, and place each job in it.\n\n:::: answer Model answer\n::: diagram Tapri's finance function, with the ten jobs placed\n{\"type\": \"nest\", \"w\": 400, \"root\": {\"title\": \"CFO\", \"dir\": \"h\", \"color\": \"mute\", \"children\": [\n {\"title\": \"Treasurer\", \"mono\": false, \"color\": \"a\", \"rows\": [[\"Cash manager\", \"1\"], [\"Fund raising\", \"2\"], [\"Capital budgeting\", \"3\"], [\"Credit manager\", \"4\"], [\"Portfolio manager\", \"5\"]]},\n {\"title\": \"Controller\", \"mono\": false, \"color\": \"d\", \"rows\": [[\"Financial accounting\", \"6\"], [\"Cost accounting\", \"7\"], [\"Tax manager\", \"8\"], [\"Data processing\", \"9\"], [\"Internal auditor\", \"10\"]]}]},\n \"caption\": \"Blue: the treasurer's managers; orange: the controller's. The number beside each manager is the job placed with them.\"}\n:::\n\nUnder the CFO (finance executive), the **treasurer** manages the money itself: the cash manager (job 1), the fund raising manager (job 2), the capital budgeting manager (job 3), the credit manager (job 4) and the portfolio manager (job 5). The **controller** manages its recording and checking: the financial accounting manager (job 6), the cost accounting manager (job 7), the tax manager (job 8), the data processing manager (job 9) and the internal auditor (job 10).\n::::\n:::::\n\n## Under the hood\n\n### Two \"controllers\" in one chapter\n\nThe Study Material uses the word in two senses. \"Role of Financial Controller\" (SM 1.11) means the head of finance, the person who takes the investment, financing and dividend decisions. In the organisation chart (SM 1.19), \"Controller\" is one of the two arms under the CFO, beside the treasurer, in charge of accounting, costing, tax, data processing and internal audit. Answer a \"role of the financial controller\" question with SM 1.11 (procurement then, the three decisions now, maximising return for the given risk), not with the chart.\n\n### Emerging issues for the CFO (an RTP extra)\n\nICAI's RTP for January 2025 (Q11(a)) asked for \"the emerging issues (any four) affecting the future role of CFO\". Its answer lists nine priorities: **regulation** (a growing personal stake in compliance); **globalisation** (a finance function that works on the global stage and embraces diversity); **technology** (\"big data\" and analytics); **risk** (more effective risk management, an appropriate corporate ethos); **transformation** (a better service to the business at zero cost impact); **stakeholder management** (the CFO as the face of the corporate brand); **strategy** (validation and execution); **reporting** (broader and burdensome requirements); **talent and capability** in the top finance role. This list is not in the Study Material edition that applies for January 2027 (the applicable RTP is the one for January 2027, not this one), so it is not core; read it once, because ICAI has asked it.\n\n### Procurement: the considerations in one line\n\nTheory question 4 at the end of the chapter asks for \"three main considerations in procuring funds\"; ICAI's answer refers to paragraph 2.1 (SM 1.29). Paragraph 2.1 names three characteristics in which every source differs, **risk, cost and control** (SM 1.5), and adds the balance between equity and debt: those are the points to write.\n\n## Exam angle\n\n::: exam Where this topic appeared\nIn the ICAI documents read for this lesson, these topics were asked 17 times, always as theory (2 to 4 marks where marks are printed):\n\n- **Inter-relationship of investment, financing and dividend decisions**, as a short note: MTP Series II for May 2024 Q4(a) and MTP Series II for September 2026 Q4(a) (4 marks each, with identical Suggested Answers); old scheme: MTP Series I for November 2019 Q6(a), RTP November 2019 Q10(c), RTP November 2023 Q10(b).\n- **The finance functions**: two basic functions, September 2025 Q4(c) (2 marks; the answer gave the definitions of I, F, D and WCM); old scheme: the three finance function decisions, November 2019 Q6(a) (3 marks) and MTP Series I for December 2021 Q6(a) (4 marks).\n- **The finance executive and the CFO**: \"discuss the role of a chief financial officer\", MTP Series I for September 2024 Q4(c) (2 marks, an OR choice, answered with the five responsibilities); key responsibilities of a finance executive in the modern business world, September 2026 (2 marks, Suggested Answers not yet published); emerging issues for the CFO, RTP January 2025 Q11(a); old scheme: the CFO in today's world, November 2020 Q6(a) (4 marks, answered with the \"now does\" column), and the finance executive's responsibilities, MTP Series I for November 2022 Q6(a) (4 marks).\n- **The financial controller**: short note, old scheme MTP Series II for November 2023 Q6(c).\n- **Importance of financial management**, as a case of a firm short of cash after expanding: RTP September 2026 Q10(a) (answered with \"money is to an enterprise what oil is to an engine\" and the seven tasks).\n- **Evolution**: the phases, old scheme December 2021 Q6(c) (2 marks).\n- **Procurement**: features of international funding and angel financing, RTP May 2026 Q10(a) (taught in [[fm.sources.modern|contemporary and international sources]]).\n:::\n\n::: exam How the marks are earned\nThe Suggested Answers copy the Study Material's lists in its order: the five responsibilities of the finance executive; the CFO's \"now does\" list; the seven tasks; the definitions of I, F and D. The inter-relationship note has a fixed shape: same objective (shareholders' wealth), so solve jointly; then one paragraph each on investment, financing and dividend; then a one-line conclusion. The case version (RTP September 2026, a firm short of cash after expanding) was answered with the importance paragraph and the seven tasks, word for word, without applying them to the facts; a line tying two or three tasks to the case's cash shortage is a safe addition.\n:::\n\n::: trick Treasurer holds the money, controller holds the records\n**Treasurer**: three Cs and two money jobs: **C**ash, **C**redit, **C**apital budgeting; **F**und raising, **P**ortfolio. **Controller**: everything that is recorded or checked: financial accounting, cost accounting, tax, data processing, internal audit. Condition: this is ICAI's chart of how the finance function *may* be organised in a large organisation (SM 1.19); a real company may organise it differently.\n:::\n\n## Answer frame\n\n### \"Discuss the role (responsibilities) of the finance executive / CFO\"\n\nOpen with one sentence: the finance executive plays an important role in the company's goals, policies and financial success. Then the five responsibilities, each with its ICAI gloss: financial analysis and planning (size and rate of growth); investment decisions (efficient allocation of funds to specific assets); financing and capital structure decisions (composition of liabilities); management of financial resources (working capital); risk management (protecting assets). If the question says \"in today's world\", add the line on the strategic business partner of the CEO and the \"now does\" list.\n\n### \"Explain the inter-relationship between investment, financing and dividend decisions\"\n\n1. The three decisions; inter-related because their objective is the same, maximisation of shareholders' wealth; consider their joint impact on the share price and solve them jointly.\n2. Investment: capital budgeting; accept only if the return at least meets the cost of financing.\n3. Financing: balance of long-term and short-term funds; mix of loan and owners' funds; the optimum mix maximises return to equity.\n4. Dividend: payout against retention; retained earnings used for internal financing deprive shareholders of dividends; optimal payout maximises wealth.\n5. Conclusion: take them jointly, keeping in view their joint effect on shareholders' wealth.\n\n### \"Distinguish the treasurer's and the controller's functions\"\n\n| Treasurer | Controller |\n|---|---|\n| Cash manager | Financial accounting manager |\n| Credit manager | Cost accounting manager |\n| Capital budgeting manager | Tax manager |\n| Fund raising manager | Data processing manager |\n| Portfolio manager | Internal auditor |\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n::: mistake Hall of Shame: the retained profit that was \"only a dividend decision\"\nBunty classifies \"retain 70% of profit to fund new cafés\" as a dividend decision and stops. **Fix:** the amount retained to support growth is also a financing decision (SM 1.9). Say both.\n:::\n\n::: mistake Answering \"role of the financial controller\" with the organisation chart\n**Fix:** SM 1.11's financial controller is the head of finance: once limited to procuring funds for major events, now taking the investment, financing and dividend decisions, maximising returns in relation to the given risk and monitoring the flow of funds. The chart's \"Controller\" is a different post.\n:::\n\n::: mistake Calling equity \"the cheapest source because there is no interest\"\n**Fix:** equity is the best source from the **risk** point of view (no repayment except in liquidation) but usually the most **expensive**: shareholders expect more than the interest rate, and dividends are not tax-deductible (SM 1.5). Debentures are cheaper but riskier.\n:::\n\n::: mistake Listing the CFO's old jobs as the \"role today\"\nAccounting, tax filing and tracking payables and receivables are in the \"used to do\" column. **Fix:** the \"now does\" list adds managing M&As, profitability and pricing analysis, outsourcing decisions, overseeing IT and HR, strategic planning, regulatory compliance and risk management, on top of budgeting and forecasting.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n::: quiz Check yourself\nQ: Case scenario. Mitti Pottery Works Ltd has ₹8 crore of profit after tax. Its board decides to pay ₹2 crore as dividend and use ₹6 crore, with a ₹4 crore bank term loan, to buy a new gas kiln. Which statement is correct?\n- [ ] Only an investment decision has been taken; the dividend and the loan follow from it automatically\n- [ ] The ₹6 crore retention is a dividend decision only, and the term loan is a working capital decision\n- [ ] The three decisions are independent, so each can be judged on its own\n- [x] The board has taken an investment decision (the kiln), a financing decision (the loan and the retention) and a dividend decision (the ₹2 crore payout), which are inter-related\n> Retention is both a dividend and a financing decision (SM 1.9). A term loan for a kiln is long-term financing, not working capital management. The decisions share one objective, shareholders' wealth, and must be taken jointly.\n\nQ: Which of the following is NOT one of the four aspects of financial management in Ezra Solomon's concept (SM 1.11)?\n- [ ] Determining the size of the enterprise and its rate of growth\n- [x] Preparing the financial statements in accordance with accounting standards\n- [ ] Determining the mix of financing, i.e. the level of debt to equity\n- [ ] Determining the composition of the assets of the enterprise\n> The fourth aspect is the analysis, planning and control of financial affairs. Preparing financial statements is accounting, an input to financial management.\n\nQ: In which phase of its evolution was financial management considered necessary only during occasional events such as takeovers, mergers, expansion and liquidation?\n- [x] The traditional phase\n- [ ] The transitional phase\n- [ ] The modern phase\n- [ ] The strategic phase\n> SM 1.7: the traditional phase; outsiders' needs (investment bankers, lenders) were kept in mind. The transitional phase gave attention to day-to-day problems; the modern phase, still going on, developed theories. There is no \"strategic phase\" in the Study Material.\n\nQ: Which of these appear in the Study Material's list of what a CFO now does but NOT in what a CFO used to do? Select all that apply.\n- [x] Managing M&As\n- [ ] Tax filing\n- [x] Pricing analysis\n- [x] Overseeing the IT function\n- [ ] Budgeting\n> Tax filing is only in the old list; budgeting and forecasting are in both. Managing M&As, pricing analysis and overseeing IT are new (SM 1.20).\n\nQ: According to the Study Material, which source of funds is the best from the firm's risk point of view, and why?\n- [ ] Debentures, because their interest is tax-deductible\n- [ ] Bank term loans, because banks also give non-fund-based services\n- [x] Equity shares, because there is no question of repaying the capital except in liquidation\n- [ ] Angel financing, because angel investors look for a higher rate of return than traditional investments\n> SM 1.5: equity is best from the risk point of view but usually the most expensive; debentures are cheaper because of the tax advantage but carry a high degree of risk. The other two options state true facts (a bank's non-fund-based services, an angel's return expectation) that say nothing about the firm's risk.\n:::\n\n## Recap\n\n- Financial management is the efficient acquisition (financing) and allocation (investment) of funds, with the objective of making profit (dividend) for owners; mainly to maximise shareholders' wealth.\n- Two aspects: procurement of funds (each source differs in risk, cost and control) and effective utilisation (fixed assets via capital budgeting, and working capital without too much blocked in it).\n- V = f(I, F, D): investment (selection of assets), financing (optimum finance, sound capital structure, profit versus cash, risk, hedging) and dividend (payout and retention, the retention being also a financing decision); short-term: working capital management.\n- The three decisions are inter-related through one objective, shareholders' wealth, and must be taken jointly.\n- Scope (Ezra Solomon): size and growth; composition of assets; mix of financing; analysis, planning and control.\n- Evolution: traditional (occasional events), transitional (day-to-day problems), modern (analysis and theories).\n- Financial controller: once procurement for major events, now the three decisions; maximise returns for the given risk. Finance executive: planning, investment, financing and capital structure, management of financial resources, risk management.\n- Under the CFO: treasurer (cash, credit, capital budgeting, fund raising, portfolio) and controller (financial accounting, cost accounting, tax, data processing, internal audit); today's CFO is the CEO's strategic partner. The matchbox has been retired."});
